<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!--
      IsPackable stays INLINE, never hoisted into shared props — the API-baseline coverage gate finds
      shipped projects by TEXT-MATCHING this element, so hoisting it would ship this package with no
      baseline and nothing would say so. Media, Compression and the mobile pair document the same trap.
    -->
    <IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
    <PackageId>Shenora.IO</PackageId>
    <Description>Shenora IO: the file-operation engine — a journalled update queue with rollback, cross-process path locks, and a staged application updater with per-file verification. Portable (net10.0), so the same code runs on every shell. Split out of Shenora.Core because an app that never mutates a file tree should not carry it.</Description>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    WHY THIS IS ITS OWN PACKAGE (2026-08-05, applying D37's test rather than overturning it).
    D37 asks: does the boundary correspond to something a CONSUMER experiences? "I am building an app
    that self-updates or mutates a file tree" does — and a phone app that hosts a page and plays media
    plainly does not, yet it carried all of this because it lived in Core, which every package
    references. That is 1,700 lines of engine — not contracts — on every consumer.

    WHY IT DEPENDS ON CORE RATHER THAN THE REVERSE, which was checked and not assumed: every type here
    logs through AppCallback, Core's one guarded-callback helper. So the edge is IO -> Core, and that
    settles what could NOT move: `Files`/`FileReplacement` stays in Core because Core's own
    IFileDialogs.SaveAsync default calls Files.BeginReplace, and PathClaims stays because it is a claim
    scope built on the mission types — scheduling vocabulary, not a file operation.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Shenora.Core\Shenora.Core.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
